{"pdfpcFormat":1,"duration":20,"disableMarkdown":false,"noteFontSize":20,"pages":[{"idx":0,"label":"1","overlay":0,"note":"Welcome to this presentation.\n\nI'm Thomas and, today, I will speak about \"Secure boot in embedded Linux systems\".\n\nBut first, I'm going to introduce myself."},{"idx":1,"label":"2","overlay":0,"note":"I'm an embedded Linux engineer and I have been a member of the Bootlin team for a few months.\n\nMoreover, I also contribute to open-source projects.\n\nI live in Southern France, close to Toulouse."},{"idx":2,"label":"3","overlay":0,"note":"This presentation is divided into two parts:\n\n- The first part introduces secure boot,\n\n- As well as some notions about the signature process.\n\n- I will also quickly talk about the impacts it can have.\n\nThe second part is an example of secure boot implementation on an i.MX8 platform, which has the particularity of not requiring an initramfs.\n"},{"idx":4,"label":"5","overlay":0,"note":"Secure boot is a feature to verify the parts used to boot a system, including hardware and software.\n\n- To protect the system against hijacking\n\n- To protect users against:\n\t- tampered applications\n\t- hardware faults\n\t- malware execution\n\nAnd finally, it allows the vendor to meet legal and commercial goals.\n"},{"idx":5,"label":"6","overlay":0,"note":"Another name for secure boot is \"Chain of trust\".\n\nBecause the main functionality of this feature is that, at boot, each stage verifies the integrity of the next stage\nbefore its execution.\n\nTo do so, each stage contains:\n\n- A signature, created at build time, that represents its integrity.\n\n- As well as the public key of the next stage, in order to perform the integrity check.\n"},{"idx":6,"label":"7","overlay":0,"note":"Here, I'm introducing the notion of asymmetric signature, used in the chain of trust.\n\nAn asymmetric signature relies on a pair of keys:\n\n- The public key that will be shared with recipients, to check the data integrity.\n\n- And the private key, which MUST be kept secret, used by the issuer to sign the shared data.\n\nThe data is hashed, then signed, and shared with recipients, in addition to the public key.\n\nThen recipients are able to verify that the digest of the received data is correct.\n"},{"idx":7,"label":"8","overlay":0,"note":"Enabling secure boot also involves being able:\n\n- to manage keys, especially to protect private keys.\n\n- to lock devices during manufacturing.\n\n- to build upgrade images including signatures and public keys.\n\nAnd to accept a longer boot time, because it adds some crypto operations to perform the checks.\n"},{"idx":8,"label":"9","overlay":0,"note":"Here, I will talk about an example of a secure boot implementation, done for an i.MX8-based target.\n"},{"idx":9,"label":"10","overlay":0,"note":"Secure boot can be implemented in several ways, depending on requirements.\n\nIn this example, there are four main parts, according to the boot process of the target:\n\n- To check the bootloader integrity, there is only one choice, which depends on the silicon vendor. Here, the feature is provided by NXP and is called Advanced High Assurance Boot.\n\n- For kernel checking, we chose to use a U-Boot feature named\n\"verified boot\".\n\n- And for the rootfs part we chose to use dm-verity because it is simple and can use hardware offloading."},{"idx":10,"label":"11","overlay":0,"note":"The Advanced High Assurance Boot is more complex than the secure boot feature implemented for previous i.MX platforms.\n\nAs with the High Assurance Boot of previous i.MX platforms:\n\n- It uses asymmetric keys and one-time programmable fuses to store the hashes of public keys.\n\n- All bootloader stages are packaged into a signed image through the Code Signing Tool.\n\n- And the secure status can be checked from U-Boot with the hab_status command.\n\n- But its boot flow is very different because it uses a Security Controller, which is also able to start a specialized processor. Moreover, it uses two containers."},{"idx":11,"label":"12","overlay":0,"note":"The image contains several binaries and is divided into two main parts:\n\n- The first contains the Security Controller firmware binary and its signature, made with NXP keys.\n\n- The second contains the SPL, the ARM Trusted Firmware, U-Boot and the associated signature, made with the OEM keys.\n\nAt boot, the System Controller Unit loads the Security Controller firmware, then the other bootloader stages.\n\nAt each step, the Security Controller validates the binary.\n\nWhen all binaries have been loaded and verified, they are executed, each in turn.\n"},{"idx":12,"label":"13","overlay":0,"note":"The first step is to generate OEM keys.\n\nKeys are generated by a script, which uses a file containing the passphrase twice and some parameters to configure the type and size of the keys and the validity period.\n\nHere, we chose to use an elliptic curve algorithm because there is a limitation in the B0 silicon of the i.MX8.\n"},{"idx":13,"label":"14","overlay":0,"note":"In addition, it's necessary to generate the hash table, as well as its checksum, which should be stored in the one-time programmable fuses.\n\nSo, there is another script for this purpose.\n\nIt's also possible to use the od command to check the hash table, in order to be sure that the values to burn are correct."},{"idx":14,"label":"15","overlay":0,"note":"Once we have the keys, the hash table and the bootloader image, it is necessary to write a small configuration file for the Code Signing Tool, in order to build the signed image.\n\nHere, you can see an example.\n\nIt contains paths to the hash table, the public key, the bootloader binary to sign.\n\nIt also contains the offsets of the signature block header, which depend on the target and can be found in the datasheet.\n\n"},{"idx":15,"label":"16","overlay":0,"note":"\nThe signed bootloader will not be verified until the fuses \nare burnt.\n\nHere is an example of the commands run from U-Boot to burn and read them.\n"},{"idx":16,"label":"17","overlay":0,"note":"Before and after burning fuses, it is also possible, from U-Boot, to check the secure boot status.\n\nIn case of an issue, the Security Controller returns an event, which can be printed with the ahab_status command.\n\nThe returned event code corresponds to a type of issue.\n\nIn addition, events are reported per container.\n\nFinally, if no event is raised, the target can be locked with the ahab_close command, which burns an additional fuse to prevent the execution of an unsigned bootloader.\n\n"},{"idx":17,"label":"18","overlay":0,"note":"Let's move on to the kernel signature.\n\nHere, we chose to use the U-Boot feature called Verified boot.\n\nThis feature uses a fitimage, which is a container whose content is described by an Image Source File.\n\nBasically, an Image Source File is a list of nodes representing each element of the fitimage.\n\nIt also uses asymmetric keys: the public key is stored in the U-Boot binary, and the private key is used to sign the kernel image at build time.\n\nThe kernel image is built and signed in three steps with the dtc and mkimage commands: building the unsigned kernel image, building the signed kernel image, and finally injecting the public key into U-Boot."},{"idx":18,"label":"19","overlay":0,"note":"A fitimage can contain several images and configurations.\n\nImages can be a kernel image, a device tree binary, a device tree overlay, boot script, and so on.\n\nA configuration is a combination of images.\n\nIn this example, the fitimage contains only one kernel image, one device tree binary and a boot script.\n\nAs well as one configuration, which combines all the provided images.\n\nMoreover, the images and configurations of the fitimage are hashed."},{"idx":19,"label":"20","overlay":0,"note":"There are two ways to sign a fitimage:\n\n- Either sign each image individually\n\n- Or sign each available configuration\n\nSigning configurations prevents mix-and-match attacks.\n\nThis example uses the second way, so a signature node has been added to the configuration node.\n\nIf we had chosen to sign each image individually, a signature node would have been added to each node describing an image."},{"idx":20,"label":"21","overlay":0,"note":"The following commands create an RSA key, as well as a public key and a certificate, which will be used to sign the kernel."},{"idx":21,"label":"22","overlay":0,"note":"Here is an example of the commands used to build and sign the kernel, and also to inject the public key into the U-Boot binary.\n"},{"idx":22,"label":"23","overlay":0,"note":"Now, it remains to take care of the rootfs integrity check.\n\nThere are several available solutions; here we chose to use dm-verity.\n\nBasically, dm-verity is a virtual file system using a hash tree, where each leaf is the hash of a block of the file system.\nIt can only be used with block devices, like squashfs, ubiblock, and so on.\nIn addition, it can only be used for read-only devices.\n\nBy default, the hash tree is stored in a separate block device, called the hash device. But it is also possible to concatenate it with the data device.\n\nIn addition, it is possible to sign the root hash."},{"idx":23,"label":"24","overlay":0,"note":"Here are a few commands to create a dm-verity device, then to check it and to open it.\n\nAs you can see, it is very easy.\n\nHere, the hash tree is appended to the end of the data image, so we use the \"--hash-offset\" parameter to specify the end of the data.\n\nIn addition, the integrity of the device can be checked without knowing the root hash, but it is necessary to mount it.\n\nThis is not the case in this example, but it is also possible to sign the root hash with a key stored in the kernel keyring."},{"idx":24,"label":"25","overlay":0,"note":"Finally, one of the requirements was to not need an additional initramfs to prepare the real rootfs device.\n\nTo do so, we use dm-init, which allows the dm-verity device containing the root file system to be mounted early, from the kernel command line.\n\nIn addition, we chose to use a boot script stored in the fitimage and loaded by U-Boot between the command that loads the kernel and the one that boots it.\n\nAs this boot script is part of the fitimage's configuration, it is also signed.\n\nThis script is useful to store the parameters required by dm-init to mount the device mapper early, like the root hash, the block size, and so on."},{"idx":25,"label":"26","overlay":0,"note":"Thank you for attending this presentation. I hope you liked it.\n\nDo you have any questions?\n"}]}